FXPlayer build instructions
****************************

This file documents how to build THIS FORK for FXPlayer's use case: aria2 with curl-impersonate's
Chrome-116 TLS/HTTP fingerprint (BoringSSL + brotli), plus the fxplayer.* RPC methods (merge/remux,
per-domain connection limiting) layered on top of upstream aria2. It supplements, not replaces, the
generic autotools INSTALL file in this directory.

Design background and the full FXPlayer <-> aria2 contract live in the FXPlayer app repo:
Docs/Aria2.md and Docs/SSL.md. This file is just the practical "how do I build it" recipe.


Normal build (fresh checkout)
==============================

    git clone <this fork's URL> aria2 && cd aria2
    ./fetch-curl-impersonate-deps.sh
    ./fetch-tdlib-deps.sh                     # optional: Telegram downloads (see below)
    autoreconf -fi
    ./configure --with-boringssl-impersonate --with-brotli-impersonate --without-gnutls [--with-tdlib]
    make -j$(nproc)

No manual dependency setup and no assumption of any pre-existing local checkout of curl-impersonate
anywhere on the machine.

fetch-curl-impersonate-deps.sh (repo root) builds the two vendored dependencies
--with-boringssl-impersonate/--with-brotli-impersonate need: a patched BoringSSL carrying Chrome 116's
TLS profile, and brotli for its certificate-compression extension. It shallow-fetches
lwthiker/curl-impersonate at a pinned commit into a gitignored scratch dir (build/curl-impersonate-src/)
-- used READ-ONLY, purely as a source of its own official patches and Makefile targets, never modified --
and drives that project's own `make` targets for just these two library sets (curl-impersonate's Makefile
already does the correct native-Linux CMake/autotools build for them; this script doesn't reimplement any
of it). curl-impersonate's own curl/nghttp2 are deliberately NOT built -- aria2 uses its own HTTP stack
and only borrows curl-impersonate's TLS backend + brotli decompression (src/LibsslTLSContext.cc,
src/LibsslTLSSession.cc). Output lands at deps/boringssl-impersonate/{lib,include}/ and
deps/brotli-impersonate/{lib,include}/ -- gitignored, and exactly the shape configure.ac expects.
Idempotent (a stamp file keyed on the pinned commit, deps/boringssl-impersonate/.built-<sha>, skips rebuilding
on a second run); pass --force to re-fetch and rebuild anyway.

--with-boringssl-impersonate/--with-brotli-impersonate accept a bare flag (no =DIR) that defaults to
deps/boringssl-impersonate / deps/brotli-impersonate respectively -- the exact paths the fetch script
populates -- so the two steps above compose with zero path bookkeeping. Pass an explicit =DIR to point at
a different vendored build instead. --without-gnutls isn't required by the BoringSSL flags themselves,
just a preference for a build that's unambiguously all-BoringSSL rather than compiling both TLS backends
in.

autoreconf -fi regenerates configure/Makefile.in from configure.ac/Makefile.am. Skip it if configure is
already present and current for your checkout (e.g. a release tarball ships a pre-generated one) --
running it is always safe either way, just occasionally unnecessary.


Telegram downloads (optional)
==============================

fxplayer.addMerge also takes a Telegram message as its source -- one segment, {"telegram": {"chatId", "msgId",
"size"?}} -- downloaded by TDLib and then remuxed (+faststart), probed and published like any other save. aria2c
can't link TDLib itself (it links the BoringSSL above; TDLib needs OpenSSL, and the two export the same symbols),
so TDLib runs in a small helper, aria2c-tg (tg-helper/), that only relays TDLib's JSON interface over a pipe.
aria2c starts it with the RPC daemon, restarts it when it exits, and closes it on shutdown (TDLib saves its state;
the helper also closes cleanly on SIGTERM, e.g. when systemd stops the service).

Build and install:

    sudo apt install gperf libssl-dev zlib1g-dev     # TDLib's build needs these (plus git/cmake/make/a C++17 compiler)
    ./fetch-tdlib-deps.sh                            # first build takes about an hour on a Raspberry Pi 5
    ./configure --with-boringssl-impersonate --with-brotli-impersonate --without-gnutls --with-tdlib
    make -j$(nproc)
    sudo make install                                # aria2c and aria2c-tg -> /usr/local/bin

fetch-tdlib-deps.sh follows fetch-curl-impersonate-deps.sh's conventions: it pins a TDLib commit (TDLIB_SHA),
shallow-fetches just that commit into build/tdlib-src/ (gitignored, never modified), builds the helper with CMake
in build/tg-helper-build/ and installs it as deps/tdlib/bin/aria2c-tg with a stamp file deps/tdlib/.built-<sha>.
A second run is a no-op; an edited tg-helper/ source is rebuilt incrementally (seconds). --force re-fetches and
rebuilds everything; JOBS=N overrides the default -j2 (TDLib's largest files need about 3 GB of memory each to
compile). TDLib has no regular release tags; its own docs build master. To update:

    ./fetch-tdlib-deps.sh --bump    # pins upstream's current master in the script, then builds it
    git diff fetch-tdlib-deps.sh    # review, then commit the new pin
    sudo make install               # (or install -m 0755 deps/tdlib/bin/aria2c-tg /usr/local/bin/) + restart aria2

--with-tdlib mirrors --with-boringssl-impersonate: a bare flag means deps/tdlib, =DIR another build (DIR/bin/
aria2c-tg), and configure fails if the helper isn't there. Without the flag `make install` still installs the
helper when it is built (and says so when it isn't); --without-tdlib never installs it. Either way the helper is
built by the script, not by make -- it is a separate program with its own toolchain (CMake, OpenSSL) and an
hour-long build -- and aria2c itself is the same binary with or without it.

aria2.conf (both options are needed; only the RPC daemon uses them):

    fx-telegram-helper=/usr/local/bin/aria2c-tg
    fx-telegram-dir=/mnt/Downloads/.tdlib    # TDLib's database (the account's session: kept 0700), its downloads,
                                             # the saved API ID/hash (fx-telegram.json, 0600), helper.log

TDLib downloads into fx-telegram-dir/files/; ffmpeg reads the finished file from there and writes the save next to
its output, so any filesystem works, but the directory needs room for the largest videos being saved. TDLib's
copy is deleted once the save is published, when the message turns out to be gone (2011), and when the save is
deleted (fxplayer.removeMergeOutput / aria2.remove) -- unless another job for the same message still needs it. A
failed or stopped download (2012/2013, fxplayer.stopMerge) keeps TDLib's partial file and the next attempt resumes
from it. helper.log is TDLib's own log (errors only); it is moved to helper.log.1 at a helper start once over 4 MB.

Sign-in: the server's Telegram account is signed in once, from the app -- saving a Telegram group with "aria2
offload" walks through it (fxplayer.telegramAuthState / fxplayer.telegramAuth): the app sends its API ID and
hash, asks for a QR login ({"qr": true}; the state becomes wait-qr with a tg://login link) and confirms that link
with its own, already signed-in TDLib. Only a two-step-verification password may then be asked (wait-password). A
phone number + code login ({"phone"}, then {"code"}) also exists. {"logOut": true} signs the server out. While
the account isn't signed in, Telegram jobs fail at once with 2012 (retryable; they don't wait).

What the log shows (log-level=warn, all one line each, tagged [fxtg]): helper started / exited (with uptime and
why -- an exit during shutdown or from a signal is a WARN, a crash an ERROR quoting helper.log's last line),
sign-in state changes and submitted steps (never their values), each download's start (gid, chat, message, size,
resume offset) and end (bytes, time, average speed; or the failure and its reason), requeues after a helper
exit, flood waits, stalls and downloads TDLib stopped, and chat-list loading. Behaviour worth knowing:
  - At most 3 Telegram downloads run at once; the rest show as `waiting` in tellStatus.
  - A helper that exits is restarted after 5 s and its downloads resume. After 3 quick exits in a row restarts slow
    to once a minute and waiting downloads fail with 2013 (e.g. a missing or broken aria2c-tg).
  - Telegram flood waits (TDLib error 429) hold the download queued for up to 10 minutes; longer ones fail (2013).
  - TDLib reports a failed download only as "stopped": aria2 asks again up to 3 times without progress, then fails
    it (2013). No new byte for 10 minutes also fails it (2013, logged as stalled after 2 minutes).
  - A chat a fresh sign-in doesn't know yet is found by loading the chat lists once (main + archive).


Jobs file (restart recovery)
=============================

Unfinished fxplayer.addMerge jobs (web and Telegram) are kept in a jobs file and added again, under the same
GIDs, when the daemon starts -- through the same code as a fresh submit, so each job's pacing, budget and
per-domain settings come back with it, and its downloaded segments (or TDLib's partial file) are reused. A job
that was merging at the restart merges again. The file defaults to fx-jobs.json next to --save-session;
--fx-jobs-file=FILE sets another path. It holds each job's request, cookies included, so it is written with mode
0600 (temporary file, fsync, rename), only when the set of jobs changes. Only the RPC daemon uses it. A file that
can't be read (corrupt, or over 256 MB) is moved aside to FILE.unreadable and no jobs are restored; a single job
that can't be added again is logged and dropped. Telegram jobs restored before the helper is up simply wait for it
(and fail with 2012 if the account turns out not to be signed in).


Rebuilding from scratch (clean slate)
========================================

To wipe every derived/generated file and rebuild from nothing -- useful after pulling upstream changes,
bumping the curl-impersonate pin, or just to confirm the build is fully reproducible:

    git status --short              # confirm nothing you care about is uncommitted first
    git clean -ndx                  # DRY RUN: preview what would be deleted
    git clean -fdx                  # actually delete it
    ./fetch-curl-impersonate-deps.sh
    ./fetch-tdlib-deps.sh           # if you use Telegram downloads: about an hour again (see below)
    autoreconf -fi
    ./configure --with-boringssl-impersonate --with-brotli-impersonate --without-gnutls [--with-tdlib]
    make -j$(nproc)

To keep the TDLib build (deps/tdlib/, build/tdlib-src/, build/tg-helper-build/), exclude it from the clean:
git clean -fdx -e deps/tdlib -e build/tdlib-src -e build/tg-helper-build (and -n first, as above).

git clean only ever removes untracked/ignored files -- it cannot touch anything committed, so your source
edits are never at risk. The -x flag matters specifically because deps/{boringssl,brotli}-impersonate/
and build/ are gitignored, and plain git clean -fd skips ignored files by default; without -x those two
directories (and configure, Makefile, config.log, config.status, src/aria2c, autom4te.cache/, etc.) would
survive the clean and you wouldn't actually be starting from a pristine tree.

IMPORTANT: git clean deletes untracked files too. If you have local changes you haven't committed yet
(including a brand-new script you just added, like fetch-curl-impersonate-deps.sh itself the first time
around), commit or at least `git add` them BEFORE running git clean -fdx, or they will be deleted along
with everything else.


Verifying the build actually works
=====================================

Version/feature banner (sanity check only -- see the note below about what it does NOT prove):

    ./src/aria2c --version

Confirm the binary is actually linked against the vendored BoringSSL, not falling back to system OpenSSL:

    grep HAVE_BORINGSSL_IMPERSONATE config.h        # should print: #define HAVE_BORINGSSL_IMPERSONATE 1
    ldd src/aria2c | grep -i 'ssl\|crypto'          # should print NOTHING (statically linked, no libssl.so/libcrypto.so)

Note on --version's own output: it prints something like "Libraries: ... OpenSSL/1.1.1g" even when
BoringSSL is what's actually linked in. That's not a bug -- BoringSSL deliberately self-reports as
"OpenSSL 1.1.1" for compatibility with code that checks that exact string. Don't use that line to judge
whether the BoringSSL build succeeded; use the two checks above instead.

Real functional test -- an actual HTTPS request through the built binary (the acceptance bar that
actually matters):

    ./src/aria2c --enable-rpc --rpc-listen-port=26800 --rpc-listen-all=false \
      --dir=/tmp/aria2-smoketest --no-conf --check-certificate=false &
    curl -s http://127.0.0.1:26800/jsonrpc -d \
      '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":"1","method":"aria2.addUri","params":[["https://www.google.com/generate_204"]]}'
    # then poll aria2.tellStatus for that gid's "status"/"errorMessage"

A successful TLS handshake shows up as the request actually reaching the server -- for this specific URL,
that's an "Unexpected status 204" error (Google's connectivity-check endpoint deliberately returns no
content, which aria2 correctly treats as nothing to download) -- NOT a TLS/connection-level error. Any
genuine certificate/handshake/connection failure would show a distinctly different error message.


Keeping in sync with FXPlayer (the iOS app)
==============================================

FXPlayer's own iOS build (Scripts/build-curl-impersonate.sh in the FXPlayer app repo) fetches
curl-impersonate the same way, at the SAME pinned commit as fetch-curl-impersonate-deps.sh in this repo --
the two must always agree, since the app and this daemon both claim to be the same browser and a mismatch
would itself be a detectable inconsistency. See Docs/SSL.md's "bumping the pinned curl-impersonate
revision" section (in the FXPlayer app repo) before changing the pinned commit in either place.
